        <div id="certifications" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What NSF/ANSI 42, 53, 401 and 58 Actually Mean
          </h2>

          <p>
            Drinking water filters in North America are certified against a short list of standards written by NSF International and the American National Standards Institute. The certification itself is done by an accredited body, usually NSF, the Water Quality Association (WQA) or IAPMO R&T. All three are legitimate. What matters is which standard the product was tested to, and which contaminants are listed under it, because a filter can be certified to Standard 53 for lead but not for PFAS, or the other way around.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">NSF/ANSI 42: taste and odor</h3>

          <p>
            Standard 42 covers aesthetic effects: chlorine taste and odor, chloramine, and particulates. It is the most common certification on cheap filters, and it is a perfectly good one if taste is your only complaint. It says nothing about health contaminants. The Waterdrop 10UA, for example, holds Standard 42 for chlorine taste and odor, which is exactly what it is good at.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">NSF/ANSI 53: health effects</h3>

          <p>
            Standard 53 is the one that matters for lead, cysts, volatile organic compounds and, under protocol P473, PFOA and PFOS. A filter certified to Standard 53 has been tested against a defined challenge level of each listed contaminant over its full rated capacity. When a product says it is certified to 53, look at which contaminants are listed. Both of our top two picks carry 53 with lead and P473.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">NSF/ANSI 401: emerging contaminants</h3>

          <p>
            Standard 401 covers so-called incidental or emerging compounds: certain pharmaceuticals, herbicides, flame retardants and BPA at the trace levels found in some tap water. It is a useful extra rather than a must-have, but it is a reliable sign that a maker paid for broad testing.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">NSF/ANSI 58 and 372</h3>

          <p>
            Standard 58 is the reverse osmosis standard, centred on total dissolved solids, and it can also list arsenic, nitrate, fluoride and other reductions. Standard 372 is often misread: it certifies that the materials in contact with water are lead-free. It does not say the filter removes lead from the water. When a listing leans on 372 and does not mention 53, it is not a lead-reduction claim.
          </p>
        </div>

