            Three patterns stand out. First, certification and flow pull in opposite directions in carbon filters. The two systems with the broadest health certification are rated at 0.5 gpm, while the fastest carbon filter on the list does not show its certification on the maker's own page. That is not a coincidence: water that moves slowly through a carbon block spends longer in contact with it, which is what makes the deeper reductions possible.
          </p>

          <p>
            Second, the cost of ownership is set by the filter life column, not the price of the system. A filter rated for 500 gallons that you replace twice a year costs more over three years than one rated for 12 months, even if the starter kit is cheaper. We look at that in more detail in the running-cost section below.
          </p>

          <p>
            Third, the two RO systems are a different category. They reduce the dissolved minerals and salts that carbon cannot touch, and they cost more, use more space and send some water to the drain. Only choose RO if your water report gives you a reason to, and if you do, the tank-or-tankless decision is mostly about cabinet space and whether there is an outlet under the sink.
