        <div id="carbon-vs-ro" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Carbon Filter or Reverse Osmosis?
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the single biggest decision in the category, and most people overbuy. Activated carbon works by adsorption: contaminants stick to the enormous internal surface of the carbon as water passes through. It is excellent at chlorine, chloramine, many organic chemicals, and, in a well-designed block, lead and PFAS. It does nothing for dissolved salts, nitrates, fluoride or hardness, because those are dissolved ions that do not bind to carbon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reverse osmosis forces water through a membrane with pores small enough to hold back most dissolved ions. That is why an RO system's certification is built around total dissolved solids, and why it can list arsenic, nitrate and fluoride reductions that no carbon filter can match. Every RO system on the market also includes carbon stages before and after the membrane, so you get the carbon benefits too.
          </p>

          <p>
            The costs of RO are real, though. It sends water to the drain while it produces filtered water; modern tankless designs such as the Waterdrop G3P800 are rated at a 3:1 pure-to-drain ratio, and older tank designs typically waste more. It takes more cabinet space, more installation time and, for tankless units, an electrical outlet. It also strips out calcium and magnesium, which is why many systems, including the iSpring RCC7AK, add a remineralization stage for taste.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical rule: on municipal water with a clean report, a certified carbon filter does everything you need. On a private well, or where the report shows nitrates, arsenic, high TDS or fluoride you want reduced, choose RO, and our <a href="/best-reverse-osmosis-system/">best reverse osmosis system guide</a> compares tank, tankless and countertop units in depth. If you are unsure, test first. A lab test costs less than the difference between the two systems, and it stops you buying the wrong one.
          </p>

          <p>
            One more distinction is worth knowing. Neither type softens water for the rest of the house. Hard water that scales your kettle will still scale your dishwasher and shower, because an under sink filter only treats one faucet. If scale on fixtures is the problem, a softener belongs on the main line, which our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">home filtration guide</a> covers in its whole-house section.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="buying-guide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Buying Guide: The Specs That Matter
          </h2>

          <p>
            Once you know whether you need carbon or RO, five specs separate a good under sink water filter system from a frustrating one. Read them in this order.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">1. Certification and the contaminant list</h3>

          <p>
            Find the standard numbers and the certifier. Then look at the contaminant list on the performance data sheet, which every certified filter is required to publish. If the contaminant you care about is not on it, the filter is not certified for it, whatever the listing says.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">2. Rated capacity in gallons</h3>

          <p>
            Certified reductions only hold up to the rated capacity. A family of four that drinks and cooks with filtered water typically uses somewhere between one and three gallons a day, which is roughly 365 to 1,100 gallons a year. Divide the filter's rated capacity by your daily use to get a realistic change interval, and use whichever comes first, the gallon limit or the time limit.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">3. Flow rate</h3>

          <p>
            A dedicated drinking faucet at 0.5 gpm is fine for glasses and kettles. If the filter feeds your main faucet through a direct connection, slow flow becomes annoying quickly, and a high-flow design such as the Frizzlife SK99-NEW makes more sense. For RO, look at the output in gallons per day and whether there is a tank to buffer it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">4. Dedicated faucet or direct connect</h3>

          <p>
            A dedicated faucet needs a spare hole in the sink or counter, or a new one drilled, but it keeps full unfiltered flow at the main tap and makes each cartridge last longer because only drinking water passes through it. A direct-connect filter uses your existing faucet, which is simpler to install and wastes cartridge capacity on dishwashing water.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">5. Cartridge cost and availability</h3>

          <p>
            Check the replacement cartridge price and part number before you buy the system. Proprietary cartridges from established brands tend to stay available for years; generic listings sometimes disappear, leaving you with a housing that nothing fits.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="installation" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Installation: What You Need Under the Sink
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most under sink carbon filters are a one-hour job with basic tools, and none of them require soldering. The usual sequence is the same across brands: shut off the cold water angle stop, open the faucet to relieve pressure, fit the supplied tee adapter between the angle stop and the faucet supply line, mount the filter bracket to the cabinet wall, and run the push-fit tubing from the tee to the filter and from the filter to the faucet.
          </p>

          <p>
            For a dedicated faucet, you need a hole. Many sinks have a spare knockout used for a sprayer or soap dispenser, and that is the easiest place to put it. If you need to drill a stone or quartz counter, that is a job for a diamond hole saw and a steady hand, or a countertop installer. Stainless sinks can be drilled with a step bit.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reverse osmosis takes longer because it needs a drain connection. A drain saddle clamps onto the sink tailpiece above the trap, and the RO's waste line connects to it. Tank systems also need floor space in the cabinet for the tank, and tankless systems need an outlet. If your sink base has a garbage disposal, a dishwasher hose and a tangle of supply lines already, measure before you order. The G3P800's slim body is a large part of its appeal in a crowded cabinet.
          </p>

          <p>
            After installation, flush the new cartridges as the manual directs, usually for several minutes, to clear carbon fines. Then check every push-fit joint for drips over the next day. A paper towel left under the fittings overnight is the simplest leak detector there is. If your sink drains slowly once everything is back in place, that is a separate problem; a <a href="/best-drain-snake/">drain snake</a> is the quickest fix.
          </p>

          <p>
            One rule applies to every filter on this page: connect it to the cold line only. Carbon filters are rated for cold water, and A.O. Smith's data sheet for the AO-US-200 states an operating range of 40 to 90°F. Hot water can damage cartridges and release what they have captured.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="running-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. The Real Cost Is the Cartridges
          </h2>

          <p>
            The starter kit price is a poor guide to what an under sink filter costs. Over five years, you will usually spend more on cartridges than on the system, so the honest comparison is the annual cartridge cost at your household's real usage.
          </p>

          <p>
            Take the two certified carbon picks. The Claryum's cartridge set is rated for 600 gallons or six months, and the AO-US-200's for 500 gallons or six months. For most households the six-month limit arrives first, so both mean two cartridge sets a year. The Waterdrop 10UA's single cartridge is rated for up to 12 months, which halves the number of changes, but you are paying less for a narrower certification. That is a fair trade if taste is the goal and a false economy if lead is the goal.
          </p>

          <p>
            Reverse osmosis has a staggered schedule. Waterdrop lists the G3P800's pre-filter at six months, its carbon block at 12 months and its membrane at 24 months. Tank systems like the RCC7AK use standard-size pre-filters that are inexpensive and widely available, with a membrane every couple of years and a remineralization cartridge on its own schedule. RO also has a hidden cost in water: whatever goes to the drain is water you pay for.
          </p>

          <p>
            Two habits keep costs down. First, buy cartridges in a two-pack or annual bundle, which most makers discount. Second, set a reminder on your phone the day you install a cartridge, because most people overrun filter life by months, and a filter past its rated capacity is no longer doing the job it was certified for. Some systems include a faucet light or timer, but a calendar reminder works for all of them.
          </p>

