        <div id="wrong-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. When an Under Sink Filter Is the Wrong Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            An under sink filter is the right tool for most kitchens, but not for every problem. Here is when to buy something else.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>You rent and cannot touch the plumbing.</strong> Even a tool-free direct-connect install changes the supply line, and some leases prohibit it. A certified pitcher or a countertop unit is the renter's answer. Our <a href="/best-water-filter-pitcher/">best water filter pitcher guide</a> ranks pitchers by the same certification rules used here, and several of them carry Standard 53 for lead.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The problem is in every tap.</strong> Rotten-egg smell from a well, iron staining in the toilet, sediment in the washing machine, or scale on every fixture are whole-house problems. Filtering one faucet does nothing for the rest. That calls for a point-of-entry system, which is covered in our <a href="/best-water-filtration-system-for-home/">home water filtration guide</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The problem is in the shower.</strong> If your complaint is dry skin, a chlorine smell when you shower, or hair that feels coated, an under sink filter will not help because it does not touch the bathroom. A shower filter is a small, cheap fix for that, with limits worth understanding; our <a href="/best-shower-head-filter/">best shower head filter guide</a> explains what they can and cannot remove.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>The water may be microbiologically unsafe.</strong> None of the carbon filters here are designed to make unsafe water safe to drink. If you are on a well that tests positive for bacteria, or under a boil-water notice, treat or boil the water as directed. Some RO systems include UV, as the G3P800 does, but a UV lamp is not a substitute for fixing a contaminated well.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>You do not know what is in your water.</strong> Buying a filter before testing is the most common mistake in this category. Test, then buy the cheapest filter certified for what you found.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Treating NSF/ANSI 372 as lead removal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Standard 372 certifies that the parts touching the water are made from lead-free materials. It says nothing about removing lead from your water. For lead reduction, look for NSF/ANSI 53 with lead named on the performance data sheet, as on the Claryum and AO-US-200, and ignore listing copy that blurs the two.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying reverse osmosis for city water that does not need it</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                RO costs more, wastes water down the drain, takes most of the cabinet and strips minerals. On municipal water with a clean report, a certified carbon filter handles chlorine, lead and PFAS. Reserve RO for wells and for reports that show high dissolved solids, nitrates, arsenic or fluoride.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running cartridges past their rated capacity</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Certified reductions only apply up to the rated gallons or months. A clogged or exhausted carbon block can stop reducing contaminants long before the flow drops noticeably. Set a calendar reminder when you install each cartridge and change it at whichever limit arrives first.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Connecting the filter to the hot line</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Under sink carbon filters are designed for cold water only. Hot water can damage the cartridge and reduce its performance. Fit the tee to the cold angle stop, and use the hot tap for washing up, not for cooking water you want filtered.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the cost of replacement cartridges</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A cheap system with expensive or short-lived cartridges costs more over five years than a pricier system with long-lived ones. Check the replacement part number, price and rated life before you buy the starter kit, and buy from a brand whose cartridges will still be sold in five years.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

