        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best under sink water filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes, the Aquasana Claryum 3-Stage. It is certified by the WQA to NSF/ANSI 42, 53 and 401, which covers chlorine taste, lead, PFOA/PFOS and many other contaminants, and it needs no tank, drain or power. If your water report shows high dissolved solids or nitrates, choose a reverse osmosis system instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are under sink water filters worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes, if you drink and cook with tap water. An under sink filter treats far more water than a pitcher at a lower cost per gallon, keeps filtered water on tap without refilling, and the certified models reduce health contaminants such as lead. They are not worth it if your water is already clean and you dislike the taste only slightly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does an under sink water filter remove lead?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if it is certified to NSF/ANSI 53 for lead. The Claryum 3-Stage and A.O. Smith AO-US-200 both carry that certification. Many cheaper filters advertise lead reduction while holding only NSF/ANSI 42 for taste and 372 for lead-free materials, which do not cover lead removal. Check the performance data sheet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should I change an under sink water filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At the rated capacity or time limit, whichever comes first. That is six months for the Claryum and AO-US-200, up to 12 months for the Waterdrop 10UA, and staggered intervals of six to 24 months for RO stages. Heavy sediment or very high chlorine can shorten that, so change sooner if the flow drops.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is reverse osmosis better than a carbon under sink filter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It removes more, but it is not automatically better. RO reduces dissolved solids, nitrates, arsenic and fluoride that carbon cannot, but it wastes water, takes more space and strips minerals. On treated city water with a clean report, a certified carbon filter usually does everything needed at a lower running cost.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I install an under sink water filter myself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Carbon filters typically take about an hour with an adjustable wrench and a drill for the bracket. You fit a tee to the cold supply, mount the filter and run push-fit tubing. A dedicated faucet needs a spare hole in the sink or counter. RO adds a drain saddle connection and, for tankless units, an outlet.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does an under sink filter reduce water pressure?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                At the filtered faucet, yes. Most certified carbon filters are rated around 0.5 gpm, and direct-connect units such as the Waterdrop 10UA at 0.75 gpm, which is slower than an unfiltered tap. High-flow designs like the Frizzlife SK99-NEW are rated up to 2.0 gpm. The rest of the house is not affected because only one line is filtered.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
